```

The `IsStableUpdated` function compares TLVs from the new data (`aTlvs`) with the existing data (`mTlvs`). The issue is that `IsStableUpdated` calls `FindPrefix(prefix->GetPrefix(), prefix->GetPrefixLength(), aTlvsBase, aTlvsBaseLength)` where `aTlvsBase` is `mTlvs` and `aTlvsBaseLength` is `mLength`.

Then it calls `FindBorderRouter(*prefixBase, true)` which iterates through the sub-TLVs of `prefixBase`. If the prefix in `mTlvs` has a BorderRouter TLV, it returns a pointer to it. Then it does `memcmp(borderRouter, borderRouterBase, borderRouter->GetLength())`.

The `borderRouter` is from the new data (attacker-controlled), and `borderRouter->GetLength()` is attacker-controlled. If the attacker sets a large length for the BorderRouter TLV in the new data, the `memcmp` would read past the end of `borderRouterBase` in `mTlvs`.

But wait, `borderRouterBase` is found in `mTlvs` which is the leader's internal buffer. The `mTlvs` buffer is `kMaxSize` (256) bytes. If the attacker sends a BorderRouter TLV with a length of, say, 200 bytes, but the existing BorderRouter TLV in `mTlvs` is only 10 bytes, then `memcmp(borderRouter, borderRouterBase, 200)` would read 200 bytes from `borderRouterBase`, which goes past the end of the actual BorderRouter TLV in `mTlvs`.

This is the stack-buffer-overflow READ! The `mTlvs` buffer is on the stack (it's part of the `NetworkData` class which is embedded in the `Leader` class which is part of the `Instance`).

